    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, our lineup is headlined by **Corbin Carroll** (76.19) and **James Wood** (73.06), who both leverage strong lineup positioning to anchor our production. **Corbin Carroll** remains a priority despite the slight penalty for Michael King\u2019s hold efficiency, as his dynamic platoon advantage against right-handed pitching provides the highest ceiling on the team. **James Wood** is similarly positioned for a massive outing; batting leadoff against Spencer Arrighetti allows him to maximize his elite .429 xwOBA, and his promotion to the top of the order adds a significant 10% boost to his efficiency score. Additionally, **Josh Naylor** (72.35) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (71.14) round out our most reliable plays, benefiting from favorable matchups against Phillips and Troy Melton, respectively, where SP skill gaps are wide enough to mitigate minor park factors.\n\nStrategic platoon edges are fueling several of our lower-tier starters. **Mickey Moniak** (63.93) is a compelling play today; while batting 9th typically drags down a profile, his 14% boost against right-handed pitching combined with Roki Sasaki\u2019s slower delivery creates a unique \"SB-hunting\" environment that outweighs the order penalty. Similarly, **Jose Caballero** (60.94) is locked into the starting nine for his high-efficiency profile against left-handed pitching, which effectively compensates for his park-adjusted baseline. The most difficult personnel decision involved **Seiya Suzuki**, who narrowly missed the starting cut with a 51.87 score. While **Seiya Suzuki** offers excellent park factors at home, the optimizer favored the raw upside and platoon-neutral efficiency of our outfield starters, ultimately opting to keep **Seiya Suzuki** on the bench to preserve our game caps.\n\nWe must remain vigilant regarding the \"Lineup Pending\" status across our roster, as our scores are predicated on assumed order placements. Any late-breaking changes\u2014particularly for **Brice Turang** or **Gabriel Moreno**\u2014could swing their projected efficiency scores if they are pushed down the order. Furthermore, while we are targeting favorable matchups, we are wary of the risk introduced by Roki Sasaki, whose delivery patterns are being exploited by **Mickey Moniak** and **Kyle Karros**. Should those specific game-flow advantages fail to materialize, we will look to pivot to depth options like **Cole Young** or **Isaac Paredes** if the late-afternoon lineups offer better stability or clearer paths to counting stats.",
